{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794729174,"nonce":"GkKb9dPAPiDPTrZ1yh+bJovdcoQ=","message":"Native writer (2026-11-15, f07644c2, fix 9a805940). crates/ash_tast/ocaml/ash_tast/ml/tast_export.ml writes the TAST file from the compiler's own types, as Export.hx does, for a whole program (no layers, positions supported but untested).\n\nOracle: tast_dump --against on the 3501-module unit suite, native vs the Haxe exporter: 8902 functions, missing 0, extra 0, differing 0 (the build-date constant of TestMain.main is allowed, as scripts/std_layer.sh does). Same program on the small test program (1099 functions) too. Counts agree: 334113 nodes, 10963 functions, 3154 classes.\n\nThe oracle found a bug in the Haxe exporter: its type cache was keyed by the compiler's text for a type, and a class's statics structure prints as Class\u003cT\u003e, as the class type does, so two different types (and any function or generic type mentioning them) shared an id. The native writer, which keys by structure, was right. Fixed in 9a805940 (types whose text mentions Class\u003c, Enum\u003c or Abstract\u003c are read structurally).\n\nCompile-time on the Mac, unit suite, 5 interleaved reps, medians: typing 2068 ms, typing + native export 2366 ms (+0.3 s), typing + Haxe macro exporter 4529 ms (+2.5 s). Inside the compiler the native build phase is 249 ms and the write 19 ms. NOT yet confirmed on the NUC (needs the OCaml toolchain there); treat as indicative.\n\nFormat layout is generated for OCaml too (gen_ocaml -\u003e tast_format.ml, a test fails when it is stale). tests/native_export.rs compares native and Haxe output when ASH_HAXE_NATIVE names a haxe with the plugin built.\n\nOpen: layers in the native writer (so the std layer is built and read natively), the NUC measurement, a driver that ships our haxe + plugin, overloads/metadata/positions as before.","files":null}]}